WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:37.000
This is 99% Invisible. I'm Roman Mars. I want you to imagine a world where you can just invent a musical instrument. I don't mean when you were a kid and you put some rubber bands on a tissue box. I mean you come up with a new instrument almost from scratch and then watch as that instrument gets taken up and played in nearly every marching band, jazz band and high school music classroom across the country. There actually was a time where this kind of thing happened. That's reporter Jay Coburn. In the 19th century, Western Europe saw an explosion of instrument innovation,
这里是 99% Invisible。我是罗曼·马尔斯。我想让你想象这样一个世界：你可以随心所欲地发明一种乐器。我不是指你小时候把几根橡皮筋套在纸巾盒上的那种。我是指你几乎从零开始构思出一种新乐器，然后看着这种乐器被全国几乎每一个行进乐队、爵士乐队和高中音乐教室所采纳并演奏。曾几何时，这种事情确实发生过。那是记者杰·科伯恩。在 19世纪，西欧迎来了乐器创新的大爆发，

2
00:00:37.000 --> 00:01:04.000
where the entire landscape of instrumentation was shifting, and where there was money to be made if your improvements caught on with the public. This is when many of the instruments we know today reached their modern forms. Trumpets had been around since Roman times, but in the 19th century, they took on the valved form we know today, and flutes went from being conical wooden instruments, to metal cylinders. But one of the greatest innovations to come out of this time
当时整个乐器界格局在不断变化，只要你的改进能得到公众的认可，就能赚到大钱。正是在这个时期，我们今天所熟知的许多乐器定型成了现代的模样。小号自罗马时期就已存在，但在 19世纪，它们演变成了我们今天所熟知的带活塞的样式，长笛也从锥形的木制乐器变成了金属圆筒。但那个时代最伟大的创新之一

3
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:30.000
was the saxophone. The saxophone wasn't an improvement on a previous instrument. It was a brand-new invention, a hybrid of brass and woodwind that not only managed to secure a spot in the musical canon, but also went on to change American music forever. Its design came from the mind of a brash young entrepreneur. His name, perhaps unsurprisingly, was sax.
就是萨克斯管。萨克斯管并不是对以往乐器的改良。它是一项全新的发明，是铜管与木管的结合体，不仅在音乐正宗曲目中争得了一席之地，而且还永远地改变了美国音乐。它的设计出自一位年轻气盛的企业家之头脑。他的名字，也许并不令人意外，就叫萨克斯。

4
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:57.000
Adolf Sachs was born in Dinole, Belgium, in 1814. His full name was actually Antoine Joseph Sax, but at the time Adolf wasn't, you know, taken. So he went by that instead. It's difficult not to remember him as this romanticized figure, the rags to riches to rags, the fiery temper. This is Dr. Stephen Cottrell, Emeritus Professor of Music at City St. George's University of London.
阿道夫·萨克斯于 1814年出生在比利时迪南。他的全名其实是安托瓦内·约瑟夫·萨克斯，但当时“阿道夫”这个名字还没怎么被人占用，所以他就直接用这个名字了。人们很难不把他当作这样一个浪漫化的人物：经历过从赤贫到暴富再到赤贫，脾气火爆。这是伦敦大学城市圣乔治分校的名誉音乐教授斯蒂芬·科特尔博士。

5
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:49.000
The brilliant innovator yet saddled with personal demons and professional challenges throughout his life. Dr. Cottrell is the author of a book called The Saxophone, but that book might never have been written because Sacks nearly died before he invented anything, like many, many times. Adolf Sacks was the eldest of 11 children, only four of whom made it to age 30, so the odds were stacked against young Adolf from the start. and I'm going to need a deep breath before I list the ways he apparently nearly died as a kid. He fell three flights of stairs and cracked his head on a stone floor. He drank a mixture of vitriol, probably what we would call sulfuric acid today and water, mistaking it for milk. He was burned in a gunpowder explosion and then again when a frying pan was knocked over.
他是一位才华横溢的创新者，但在其一生中却背负着个人心魔和职业挑战。科特尔博士是《萨克斯管》一书的作者，但这本书可能永远都不会问世，因为萨克斯在发明任何东西之前差一点就死掉了，而且是很多很多次。阿道夫·萨克斯是 11个孩子中的老大，其中只有 4人活到了 30岁，所以从小对小阿道夫来说，命运多舛。在我列举他小时候显然差点送命的种种方式之前，我需要先深吸一口气。他从三层楼的楼梯上摔下来，脑袋在一楼的石地板上摔裂了。他误把稀硫酸（大概就是我们今天所说的硫酸）和水的混合物当成牛奶喝了下去。他在火药爆炸中被烧伤，后来平底锅被打翻时又再次被烧伤。

6
00:02:49.000 --> 00:03:16.000
A stone fell off a roof and left a lifelong scar on his head and he once fell asleep in a room full of freshly varnished furniture, but was luckily found before he succumbed to the fumes. I think what we're getting here is a picture of somebody who is energetic, getting into scrapes, whether it's falling downstairs, whether it's going to sleep in a room where varnish is drying, whether it's having his head cracked open. Some of these stories might be apocryphal,
一块石头从屋顶掉下来，在他头上留下了终生难忘的伤痕，有一次他睡在摆满新漆家具的房间里，幸运的是在被毒气熏死之前被人发现了。我认为我们在这里看到的是一个精力充沛的人的形象：他总是磕磕绊绊，无论是从楼梯上摔下来、在油漆正干的房间里睡着，还是脑袋被砸裂。其中有些故事可能是虚构的，

7
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:48.000
but this long list of mishaps is probably why his mother once said that, quote, he's a child condemned to misfortune. He won't live. Thanks, Mom. She was right about the first part, but not about the second, because, against all odds, Adolf did survive his tragicomic childhood. And as a young adult, he joined his father in the family business, making musical instruments. Adolf's father, Charles Joseph Sachs, had gotten into instrument making because he was a musician himself.
但这一长串的倒霉事，大概就是为什么他的母亲曾说过，引述：“他是一个命中注定多灾多难的孩子。他活不长的。”谢谢你，妈妈。她前半句说对了，但后半句错了，因为历经艰难险阻，阿道夫确实挺过了他那喜剧悲剧交织的童年。在青年时期，他加入了父亲的家族企业，从事乐器制造。阿道夫的父亲查尔斯·约瑟夫·萨克斯之所以涉足乐器制造，是因为他本人就是个音乐家。

8
00:03:48.000 --> 00:04:05.000
His professional training had been as a carpenter, but when he joined a woodwind band in Dinant, he didn't have an instrument. so he taught himself to make one. That must have gone pretty well because soon he moved to Brussels and set up an instrument shop. That's where young Adolf Sachs learned his craft. I think we have to remember
他受过木匠的专业培训，但当他加入迪南的一个木管乐队时，他没有乐器，于是便自学做了一把。那一定进展得相当顺利，因为不久后他就搬到布鲁塞尔开了一家乐器店。年轻的阿道夫·萨克斯就是在那里学会了他的手艺。我想我们必须记住

9
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:28.000
we're now talking early 19th century. Musical instrument making in Europe at this time was not done in big factories. There was no industrialisation involved. It was small, artisanal, individual craftsmen. It's much more a craft-oriented tradition. Young Adolf clearly felt the pull of opportunity. As a teen, he was enrolled in the Royal School of Music in Brussels.
我们现在谈论的是 19世纪初。当时欧洲的乐器制造并非在大型工厂里进行。其中不涉及工业化。它是小型的、手工的、由个体工匠完成的。它更像是一个以手艺为导向的传统。年轻的阿道夫显然感受到了机遇的召唤。青少年时期，他就读于布鲁塞尔皇家音乐学院。

10
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:59.000
He was an impressive musician, a virtuoso on the flute and clarinet. When he was about 20, one composer even dedicated a clarinet piece to Adolf Sax. There was a promising career as a musician waiting for him, but he was lured back to the family instrument workshop. While working alongside his dad, Adolf started experimenting with the instrument he knew best, the clarinet. Specifically, the bass clarinet. The bass clarinet was kind of a quirky novelty at the time.
他是一位令人印象深刻的音乐家，也是长笛和单簧管的演奏大师。大约 20岁时，甚至有一位作曲家专门为阿道夫·萨克斯创作了一首单簧管曲。一条大有可为的音乐家生涯正等待着他，但他又被吸引回了家族的乐器作坊。在与父亲一起工作的同时，阿道夫开始对最熟悉的乐器——单簧管进行试验。具体来说，是低音单簧管。低音单簧管在当时算是一种古怪的新奇玩意儿。

11
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:25.000
It was difficult to play and sounded kind of nasal, and it was so long that it was difficult to get your fingers in the right place. It looked a bit like a folded bassoon. So Adolf Sax experimented with new keywork, the metal parts that you press to close the holes. He also used the science of acoustics to make the placement of those holes more precise. People would put these boreholes, in more or less wherever they felt it looked about right.
它很难演奏，声音听起来有点像鼻音，而且太长了，手指很难按到正确的位置。它看起来有点像折叠的大管。于是阿道夫·萨克斯尝试了新的按键系统——即按下以关闭音孔的金属部件。他还运用声学科学使这些音孔的位置更加精准。人们以前制作这些钻孔时，基本上是觉得哪里看起来差不多合适就开在哪里。

12
00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:55.000
He came up with the measurements. He decided to make this a much more scientific operation than previously in determining where the holes actually needed to be cut. This improvement meant that each instrument sounded more in tune. And when played together, the bass clarinet sounded more harmonious. And so, in 1835, in his early 20s, Adolf Sachs took his bass clarinet to an instrument show. And 19th century Belgian instrument aficionados loved it.
他想出了测量标准。在确定实际需要切割音孔的位置时，他决定让这项工作比以往更加科学。这一改进意味着每种乐器的音准都更好。当一起演奏时，低音单簧管听起来更加和谐。因此，在 1835年，二十出头的阿道夫·萨克斯带着他的低音单簧管参加了一个乐器展。19世纪的比利时乐器爱好者们对此爱不释手。

13
00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:30.000
In fact, the bass clarinets used in orchestras today still owe their design to Adolf Sax, a wooden tube covered in keys that curves into a metal bell shape at the bottom end. The top end crooks into a mouthpiece with a wooden reed to blow on. In other words, the bass clarinet looks a lot like the saxophone eventually would. With his redesign of the bass clarinet, the name Saxes was beginning to be known in musical circles. But if he really wanted to make the big francs, he had to move to Paris. Brussels were very small at the time, and Paris was one of the major musical centres
事实上，如今管弦乐队中使用的低音单簧管仍然归功于阿道夫·萨克斯的设计：一根覆盖着按键的木管，底部弯曲成金属喇叭口状。顶部则弯曲成带有木质簧片的吹口供吹奏。换句话说，低音单簧管看起来非常像后来的萨克斯管。通过对低音单簧管的重新设计，“萨克斯”这个名字开始在音乐界崭露头角。但如果他真的想赚大钱，他就必须搬到巴黎。当时布鲁塞尔非常小，而巴黎则是欧洲主要的音乐中心之一。

14
00:06:30.000 --> 00:07:24.000
in Europe. And Paris was particularly the centre of brass wind manufacturer. You had to be seen to be producing your new brass and wind instruments if you were going to be successful. In 1843, Adolf Sachs set up a workshop there and got to experimenting. He came up with new families of horns, the sax horns, which were valve brass instruments that looked a bit like upright trumpets, and the saxotrumbas, which were valved brass instruments that looked a bit like upright trumpets. There are videos of people playing these on the internet. This is the sax horn. And this is the saxo tromba. And perhaps it's worth noting here that Adolf Sachs didn't really invent these instruments,
巴黎尤其是铜管乐器制造的中心。如果你想取得成功，你就必须让人看到你正在生产新的铜管和管乐器。1843年，阿道夫·萨克斯在那里建立了一个车间并开始进行试验。他发明了新的号角系列：萨克斯号（sax horns，这是一种活塞铜管乐器，看起来有点像立式小号）和萨克斯特龙巴号（saxotrumbas，也是带活塞的铜管乐器，看起来有点像立式小号）。互联网上有人们演奏这些乐器的视频。这是萨克斯号。这就是萨克斯特龙巴号。也许这里值得一提的是，阿道夫·萨克斯并没有真正发明这些乐器，

15
00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:45.000
so much as improve on things that already existed. And then added sacks to the name. If you asked Adolf, though, he'd probably tell you this was a mere technicality. Don't be such a sax down air. He'd say he was here to disrupt the industry. Move fast and break things. Sax style. Although Adolf was turning out a variety of instruments,
与其说是发明，不如说是对现有事物的改进。然后把“萨克斯”这个名字加了进去。不过，如果你去问阿道夫，他可能会告诉你这只是个技术细节。别这么像个萨克斯扫兴鬼。他会说他是来颠覆这个行业的。快速行动，打破常规。萨克斯风格。尽管阿道夫生产了各种各样的乐器，

16
00:07:45.000 --> 00:08:33.000
None of them had been a big enough hit to make him properly rich. He was one instrument maker among many in Paris, all competing for the attention of orchestras and private buyers. So he came up with a plan to secure his own niche in a competitive market. He went after a client so huge, so wealthy that their business could set him up for life. Because since time immemorial, there's been one tried and true way for an entrepreneur to make a ton of money. Adolf Sachs wanted to land a military contract. If you could get a contract with the French military to supply brass instruments or brass wind instruments, then that was going to be the backbone of your business. You were going to sell far more instruments to the French military than you ever were going to the orchestras in Paris.
但没有哪一种火到能让他真正发财的地步。他是巴黎众多乐器制造商中的一员，所有人都在争夺管弦乐队和私人买家的注意力。因此，他在竞争激烈的市场中想出了一个确保自己一席之地的计划。他瞄准了一位如此庞大、如此富有的客户，以至于他们的生意能让他受用终生。因为自古以来，企业家赚大钱都有一个屡试不爽的方法。阿道夫·萨克斯想要拿下军方合同。如果你能与法国军方签订合同，为他们供应铜管乐器或铜管风鸣乐器，那将成为你业务的顶梁柱。你向法国军方卖出的乐器数量，远超你在巴黎的管弦乐队身上能卖出的数量。

17
00:08:33.000 --> 00:09:14.000
If cannons and cavalry were how a country demonstrated military prowess, then music was how it showed its cultural might. And France was lagging behind. Austria and Prussia's military bands were leading the way with fancy new valve technology in their brass instruments, which made for loud, impressive displays. France's military band, on the other hand, was still dominated by woodwind instruments, which are tuneful and elegant, but not loud enough to fill a parade ground. In 1845, the French Ministry of War established a commission to investigate improvements to military bands, and Sachs decided that he was going to hit it big by designing the purpose.
如果说大炮和骑兵是一个国家展示军事实力的方式，那么音乐就是它展现文化力量的方式。而法国当时却落后了。奥地利和普鲁士的军乐队在铜管乐器中率先采用了新颖华丽的活塞技术，从而呈现出响亮、震撼的展示效果。相比之下，法国军乐队仍然由木管乐器主导，虽然它们音色优美、典雅，但音量不足以填满阅兵场。1845年，法国战争部成立了一个委员会来调查如何改进军乐队，萨克斯决定通过设计专门的产品来一鸣惊人。

18
00:09:14.000 --> 00:10:06.000
perfect instrument to lift French military bands out of the toilettes. Adolf thought he could have the best of both brass and woodwinds, with a little tinkering. His starting point was this other instrument that was common in bands at the time, called the Offerclide. It's a little bit difficult to describe. If you turned a trumpet on its end with the bell pointing up to the sky and then made it about 30 times bigger, you've got the general shape of an Offerck. Clyde. The Offerclide had this going for it. It was loud, but other than that, it was pretty much a dud. It's a big honking instrument that was difficult to play in tune. This is a recording of an Offer Clyde I found online.
旨在将法国军乐队从颓势中拯救出来的完美乐器。阿道夫认为，通过一点小小的改进，他就能兼具铜管和木管乐器的优点。他的出发点是当时乐队中常见的另一种乐器，叫做奥菲克莱号（ophicleide）。这有点难以描述。如果你把一个小号竖起来，让喇叭口指向天空，然后把它放大约 30倍，你就能大致想象出奥菲克莱号的形状。奥菲克莱号有这样一个优点：它声音很大，但除此之外，它基本上是个失败品。它是一个声音巨大、刺耳且很难按准音高演奏的乐器。这是我在网上找到的奥菲克莱号的录音。

19
00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:51.000
The composer Hector Berlioz said, the quality of these low sounds is rude. It is as if a bull escape from its stall had come to play off its vagaries in the middle of a drawing room. The Offerclade was usually part of the low bass end of the orchestra, where the tuba sits today. And so Sacks saw this area of the bands and the orchestras as needing attention, and he therefore looked at the offerclade, and he experimented with the Offerclade. Adolf Sax borrowed from his earlier project, the bass clarinet, and created a sort of hybrid brass and reed instrument. He quite literally took the brass mouthpiece that was on the Offer Clyde and replaced it with a bass clarinet mouthpiece.
作曲家埃克托·柏辽兹曾说过：“这些低音的音质很粗糙。就好像一头从牛棚里逃出来的公牛，跑到客厅正中央去撒野一样。”奥菲克莱号通常属于管弦乐队的低音部分，也就是今天大号所坐的位置。因此，萨克斯认为乐队和管弦乐队的这个区域需要关注，于是他将目光投向了奥菲克莱号，并对奥菲克莱号进行了试验。阿道夫·萨克斯借鉴了他先前的项目——低音单簧管，创造了一种类似铜管与簧片的混合乐器。他字面意义上取下了奥菲克莱号上的铜制吹口，换上了一个低音单簧管吹口。

20
00:10:51.000 --> 00:11:17.000
That's what we believe he did in the late 1830s, and the reed mouthpiece would make a smoother, softer sound. It would make a richer harmonic spectrum. Adolf also placed the boreholes scientifically, the way he had with the bass clarinet. Crucially, this wasn't just one instrument. It was a whole family. Adolf Sax was looking to improve the entire ensemble,
这就是我们认为他在 19世纪 30年代末所做的事情，而簧片吹口会发出更平稳、更柔和的声音。它会产生更丰富的谐波频谱。阿道夫还像处理低音单簧管那样，以科学的方式安置了音孔。至关重要的是，这不单单是一种乐器。而是一个完整的家族。阿道夫·萨克斯旨在改进整个乐队，

21
00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:48.000
and so he created families of similar instruments at different pitches. He'd already done this with the saxhorns and saxotrombas a few years before. The idea was to create a chorus of instruments working together, a little like a choir of human voices, with altos, tenors, sopranos, and bass all working together in harmony. And just like his saxhorns and saxotrombas, he named them after himself, the saxophones. Adolf Saxe patented his family of saxophones in 1846.
因此他创造了音高不同、却彼此相似的乐器家族。几年前他已经在萨克斯号和萨克斯特龙巴号上这样做了。这个想法是创造出一组合奏的乐器，有点像人声合唱团，中音、次中音、女高音和低音全部和谐地协同工作。就像他的萨克斯号和萨克斯特龙巴号一样，他以自己的名字为它们命名——萨克斯管（saxophones）。阿道夫·萨克斯于 1846年为他的萨克斯管家族申请了专利。

22
00:11:48.000 --> 00:12:20.000
There are eight figures on that patent, ranged by size and pitch. Some of them are straight. Some are more like an offaclide. But right at the center of that lineup was an instrument with a bell-shaped horn, a reed mouthpiece, and an iconic S-shaped. Adolf had created a brand-new kind of instrument, one that was light and sturdy. The S-shaped in particular was easy to hold while marching. It was loud enough to carry over a parade ground but with the rich harmonics and refined tone of a clarinet.
该专利图上有八个图形，按大小和音高排列。有些是直的。有些更像奥菲克莱号。但在这个阵容的正中央，是一种带有喇叭状号筒、簧片吹口以及标志性 S形的乐器。阿道夫创造了一种崭新的乐器，轻巧而坚固。特别是那个 S形，在行进时很容易拿握。它的音量足够大，足以盖过阅兵场，同时又具有长笛那般丰富的谐波和精致的音色。

23
